      "text": "To determine the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we first need to understand the periodicity of the individual components \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\).\n\nThe function \\(\\tan x\\) has a period of \\(\\pi\\) because \\(\\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x\\). Similarly, the function \\(\\cot x\\) also has a period of \\(\\pi\\) because \\(\\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x\\).\n\nWhen we sum these two functions, \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to find the smallest positive value \\( T \\) such that applying the transformations \\(\\tan\\) and \\(\\cot\\) to an input \\( x + T \\) results in an output that is equivalent to applying them to \\( x \\). This means we need to find the smallest \\( T \\) such that:\n\\[ \\tan(x + T) + \\cot(x + T) = \\tan x + \\cot x. \\]\n\nSince \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\) both have a period of \\(\\pi\\), we need to find a value of \\( T \\) that is a multiple of \\(\\pi\\) such that the periodicity condition is satisfied. To do this, let's consider the function:\n\\[ \\tan(x + \\pi) + \\cot(x + \\pi) = \\tan x + \\cot x. \\]\nThis is true because:\n\\[ \\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x. \\]\nTherefore, the smallest positive \\( T \\) that satisfies this condition is \\(\\pi\\).\n\nTo confirm, let's check if \\( T = 2\\pi \\) also works:\n\\[ \\tan(x + 2\\pi) + \\cot(x + 2\\pi) = \\tan x + \\cot x. \\]\nBut \\(\\tan(x + 2\\pi) = \\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot(x + 2\\pi) = \\cot x\\), so:\n\\[ \\tan(x + 2\\pi) + \\cot(x + 2\\pi) = \\tan x + \\cot x, \\]\nwhich is not the same as \\(\\tan x + \\cot x\\). Therefore, \\( T = 2\\pi \\) does not work.\n\nThe only value of \\( T \\) that works is \\( T = \\pi \\). Therefore, the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi}\n\\]",
