      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we first need to understand the periods of \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\) individually. The function \\(\\tan x\\) has a period of \\(\\pi\\), meaning \\(\\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x\\). Similarly, the function \\(\\cot x\\) also has a period of \\(\\pi\\), meaning \\(\\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x\\).\n\nSince the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) involves both \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\), the period of \\( y \\) will be the smallest positive value \\( T \\) such that \\( y(x + T) = y(x) \\) for all \\( x \\). This means that both \\(\\tan(x + T)\\) and \\(\\cot(x + T)\\) must equal \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\), respectively. Therefore, we need:\n\n\\[\n\\tan(x + T) = \\tan x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cot(x + T) = \\cot x\n\\]\n\nThis implies that \\( T \\) must be a multiple of both \\(\\pi\\) for \\(\\tan(x + T) = \\tan x\\) and a multiple of \\(\\pi\\) for \\(\\cot(x + T) = \\cot x\\). The smallest positive value \\( T \\) that satisfies both conditions is \\(\\pi\\). To verify, we check:\n\n\\[\n\\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x\n\\]\n\nThis means that \\( y(x + \\pi) = \\tan(x + \\pi) + \\cot(x + \\pi) = \\tan x + \\cot x = y(x) \\). Therefore, the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi}\n\\]",
